  1. THE PUBLIC SURFACE OF THE OLD MODULE. Read the module being replaced and
     list every name it exported. Anything a caller uses that is absent above
     must be re-added, adapted to the pair, before this ships - a rewrite that
     quietly drops a helper breaks a call site that has no test.

  2. EVERY CALL SITE. Search the whole repository for callers of the dedup
     module, not just the two named here. Sections 3.2 and 3.3 are the two the
     documentation records; the search is what finds the third.

  3. THE PERSISTED FILE NAME AND LOCATION. The path is passed in above; confirm
     the caller passes the same one the previous module used, so the legacy
     migration in _adopt_legacy actually sees the old file. If the path
     changed, the migration silently does nothing and the current window
     becomes claimable again.

  4. THE BOT IDENTIFIER IS INJECTED, SHORT AND OPAQUE. It ends up in a state
     file on disk. It must not be the token, a prefix of the token, the bot
     username, or a numeric identifier from the provider.

  5. THE WINDOW IS AT LEAST THE PROVIDER'S REDELIVERY WINDOW. Pruning or
     bounding shorter than the interval over which the provider may retry
     re-opens the replay window this module closes. Check the documented
     retry behaviour and set DEFAULT_WINDOW_PER_BOT, or the injected bound,
     above it.

  6. THE STATE FILE DIRECTORY IS WRITABLE BY THE SERVICE USER, and is on the
     same filesystem as the temporary file - which it is, by construction
     above, provided the path is not a symbolic link across a mount.

  7. A CONCURRENT SECOND WRITER WOULD BREAK THIS. This module is safe against a
     crash, not against two processes claiming into one file. If the ASGI
     server from change 001 and the stdlib server or the poller can run at the
     same time, exactly one of them may hold the dedup state. Decide that
     explicitly.

